1. INTRODUCTION: WHY INVOCATION IS DANGEROUS

1.1 The Isolation Property

Base ST-T1024 (standard §2.1) declares N-independent tiles. Each tile:

· Has its own ROM and SRAM.
· Executes in its own power domain.
· Is activated alone (at most one tile powered at a time).

This is the isolation property. It is what makes the system certifiable under ISO 26262 (freedom from interference) and IEC 62443 (zones).

1.2 What Invocation Threatens

Invocation means tile A requests computation from tile B. Without discipline, this introduces:

Threat Mechanism
State leakage B reads A's state or vice versa
Context confusion B executes assuming A's context
Power-domain violation A and B both active, breaking single-active isolation
Unbounded recursion A calls B calls A calls …
Untraceable delegation No audit trail of who invoked whom
Denial of service B's failure cascades to A without recourse

A6 must close all six.

1.3 The Design Principle

Invocation is a message, not a shared memory. The caller and callee never coexist in the same power domain. The call is verifiable, bounded, and logged.

