2. THE INVOCATION GRAPH

2.1 Definition

Definition 1 (Invocation Graph). The invocation graph G = (T, E) is a directed graph where:

· T is the finite set of tiles (A4 §2.1).
· E ⊆ T × T is the set of declared invocations.

An edge (A, B) ∈ E means: tile A is permitted to invoke tile B.

2.2 ROM-Burned

Rule G1 (Immutability). G is burned into ROM at manufacture. It does not change at runtime.

Rule G2 (Declared Edges). Only edges in E are permitted. Any invocation not matching a declared edge is refused with 0x-INVALID-INVOCATION.

2.3 The DAG Property

Definition 2 (Acyclic). G is a DAG: there is no cycle in E.

Rule G3 (DAG-Only). G must be acyclic. Graphs with cycles are rejected at ROM burn time.

Rationale. A cycle in G would allow unbounded invocation chains. The DAG property is what makes invocation depth bounded.

2.4 Reachability and Depth

Definition 3 (Invocation Depth). For a path A → B → C in G, the depth is the number of edges: depth(A → B → C) = 2.

Definition 4 (Maximum Depth). D_invoke is the maximum depth over all paths in G:

```
D_invoke = max { depth(p) : p is a path in G }
```

Rule G4 (Bounded Depth). D_invoke must satisfy D_invoke ≤ D_invoke_max, a standard constant (recommended: 3).

2.5 The Graph Finiteness Property

Proposition A6-G (Finiteness). |E| is finite and D_invoke is finite.

Proof. T is finite (A4 §2.1). E ⊆ T × T, so |E| ≤ |T|² < ∞. Since G is a DAG, all paths are finite. Since T is finite and G is a DAG, the maximum path length is bounded by |T|. Therefore D_invoke ≤ |T| < ∞. □

