5. ISOLATION GUARANTEES

5.1 The Three Isolation Domains

Domain Guarantee Enforcement
Power At most one tile active at a time Phase 3 + Phase 6 gating
Memory No shared mutable state Read scope + context hash
Control Caller and callee have separate FSMs Sequential protocol

5.2 Isolation Preservation Theorem

Theorem A6-I (Isolation Preserved). During the invocation protocol, at no point do the caller and callee share power, mutable memory, or control flow.

Proof.

· Power: By Phase 3, the caller powers down before Phase 4 begins. By Phase 6, the callee powers down before the caller powers up. The single-active property holds throughout.
· Memory: By Rule M4, the callee reads only the declared read-scope projection. By Rule P2, the callee cannot read the caller's live state, because the caller is powered down. The read-scope projection is copied into the invocation message and hash-verified. No shared mutable memory exists.
· Control: The caller's FSM is suspended during Phase 4 (its power domain is off). The callee's FSM runs in its own domain. No interleaving occurs.

Therefore isolation is preserved throughout the protocol. □

5.3 Relation to the Base Isolation Property

The base architecture's isolation property (standard §2.1) says: "at most one tile powered at a time."

A6 preserves this property. It does not weaken it.

The invocation protocol is a sequential handoff between tiles, not a concurrent execution. At every instant, exactly one tile is active, and the active tile is unambiguous.

---

6. FAILURE AND REFUSAL

6.1 Failure Modes

Failure Cause Effect
0x-INVALID-INVOCATION Edge not in E Caller refuses to send
0x-CONTEXT-MISMATCH Hash mismatch Callee refuses to admit
0x-DEPTH-EXCEEDED depth + 1 > D_invoke Caller refuses to send
0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED Sub-task exceeds budget Callee aborts, returns FAILED
0x-CALLEE-REFUSED Callee's precondition fails Callee returns REFUSED
0x-CALLEE-TIMEOUT Sub-task exceeds WCET Callee aborts, returns FAILED

6.2 Caller Response

On any failure, the caller applies the plan's declared failure policy (A3 §7.2):

· Halt — caller transitions to REFUSE_ACT.
· Retry(n) — caller re-sends the invocation, up to n times.
· RollbackTo(step) — caller restores a prior checkpoint.
· Skip — caller continues to next sub-task (discouraged).

Rule F1 (Bounded Retries). Retry count is bounded by the plan's declared policy. Total cycles consumed, including retries, must not exceed the task budget.

6.3 Callee Independence

Rule F2 (Callee Persistence). The callee's state is not modified by a failed invocation. The callee's SRAM, ROM, and invariants remain as before.

This preserves isolation: caller failures do not corrupt callee state.

