7. THE CALL STACK (REDUX)

7.1 The Stack

A1 §4 defines the call stack. A6 provides the semantics:

· PUSH occurs at Phase 1 (invocation sent).
· POP occurs at Phase 6 (invocation returned).
· PEEK is used by the callee's admit conditions (Phase 2).

7.2 Stack Frame

Definition 8 (Frame). A stack frame is:

```
frame = ⟨ caller_id, callee_id, plan_id, step_index, checkpoint_hash ⟩
```

All fields are finite. The stack depth is bounded by D_invoke.

7.3 Stack Bound

Proposition A6-S (Stack Bounded). |stack| ≤ D_invoke + 1.

Proof. By Rule G4, invocation depth is bounded by D_invoke. Each invocation adds one frame. By Rule M3, no invocation is admitted if it would exceed the bound. Therefore the stack depth is bounded by D_invoke + 1 (the +1 accounts for the initial frame). □

---

8. FORMAL PROPERTIES

The properties A6 contributes to the Verifiable Agency Theorem:

Property A6.1 (Graph Finiteness). |E| < ∞ and D_invoke < ∞.

Proof. Proposition A6-G. □

Property A6.2 (DAG Property). G is acyclic.

Proof. Rule G3, enforced at ROM burn time. □

Property A6.3 (Isolation Preservation). Invocation preserves tile isolation.

Proof. Theorem A6-I. □

Property A6.4 (Invocation Termination). Every invocation chain terminates.

Proof. By DAG property (A6.2) and depth bound (A6.1), every invocation chain has finite length. Each step is bounded by its budget. Therefore every chain terminates. □

Property A6.5 (Hash Integrity). Context hash verification guarantees read-scope fidelity.

Proof. Rule M5. Any mismatch is refused. □

These are the A6 obligations. The Verifiable Agency Theorem's assumption A6 (§5.1 of the theorem) is grounded.

