10. OPEN DESIGN TENSIONS

# Question Impact
1 Hash function choice. SHA-256 is standard but not ternary-native. Is a ternary-compatible hash required? Affects hardware implementation cost.
2 Read scope declaration. Per-edge or per-tile-pair? Per-edge is more precise; per-pair is simpler. Affects ROM size and graph expressiveness.
3 Return value size. If the callee's result is large, it may exceed a single message. Is chunking allowed? If not, sub-tasks must return bounded results.
4 Callee precondition checks. Phase 2 §4.2 item 5 checks callee invariants. But the callee has no visibility into the caller's intent. How does it check? May require the invocation message to carry caller-intent metadata.
5 Cross-domain invocation. Can MATH invoke LAW? Or is invocation restricted to same-class tiles? Affects graph shape.
6 Concurrent invocations. A plan step may need two independent sub-tasks. Can the caller invoke B and C sequentially, or is parallelism needed? Sequential only (Rule P2). Parallelism would break single-active isolation.
7 Recovery from callee crash. If the callee fails to return (power loss, hardware fault), what does the caller see? Needs a timeout mechanism at the caller.
8 The depth accounting. Is depth measured at the caller or callee? Affects Rule M3 enforcement.

None are fatal. All must be resolved before A6 v2.0.

